I plan to work out why decisions get lost before I pick a memory design. The research so far points two ways. Some losses come from compaction throwing the decision away. Others happen while the decision is still in the context window and the agent just doesn't use it. Each needs a different fix.

**Where the bar stands (from searches; the papers aren't read yet)**
- **No direct comparison exists.** I found no controlled study of compaction against retrieval memory on long coding sessions. The claims that compaction is the problem come from blogs ([Codex CLI ACM post](https://codex.danielvaughan.com/2026/08/02/acm-agentic-context-management-long-horizon-tasks-codex-cli-compaction-external-memory-retrieval/), [Tessl on Amp retiring compaction](https://tessl.io/blog/factory-publishes-framework-for-evaluating-context-compression-in-ai-agents/)).
- **Closest coding benchmark:** [LoCoEval](https://www.alphaxiv.org/abs/2603.06358) reports models scoring under 50% at pulling out details even when the whole conversation fits in the window. That suggests a bigger window alone won't fix our problem. It also proposes a memory add-on, Mem0_R.
- **Learned compaction:** [REMORY](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.11287) reports gains on Terminal-Bench 2.1.
- **Chat memory, not coding:** on [LongMemEval](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2410.10813), vendors report 71–97% ([Zep](https://blog.getzep.com/state-of-the-art-agent-memory/), [Emergence](https://www.emergence.ai/blog/emergence-is-the-new-new-state-of-the-art-in-agent-memory), [Redis](https://redis.io/agent-memory-benchmark-report/), [Supermemory](https://supermemory.ai/research)). Each uses a different setup, so these scores can't be compared with each other.
- **Ours:** decisions are lost after about 150 turns across 40 sessions. That is a cutoff point, not a rate, so the first step is to turn it into one.

**First steps**
1. **Rebuild the baseline from the 40 sessions.** For each session I'll list the decisions made and check whether each one was later recalled or contradicted. That gives a recall rate by turn bucket (0–50, 50–100, …, 300+), with the count of decisions per bucket and confidence intervals. I'll also count sessions under 150 turns and decisions that were never tested, rather than dropping them.
2. **Classify every miss.** Each lost decision is either:
   - **evicted:** gone from the context, through compaction or truncation;
   - **present but ignored:** still in the window but not used;
   - **overridden:** replaced by a later instruction.

   This split decides the approach. I'll do this diagnosis before calling the cutoff unexplained.
3. **Read the primary sources** (the methods sections of LoCoEval, REMORY and MemoryAgentBench) before relying on any of their numbers.

**What I'll measure, and against what**
I'll replay the same 40 sessions with probe questions placed at fixed turns, comparing each approach with the current system on the same sessions:
- (A) the current compaction;
- (B) a structured decision ledger: the agent writes each decision to a file that is re-injected into its context every turn;
- (C) retrieval memory in the Mem0 style;
- (D) a handoff summary in place of compaction.

For each approach I'll record:
- decision recall at turns 150, 300 and 500;
- contradiction rate;
- task success;
- tokens and time per turn;
- regressions.

All runs use the same model and budgets, and I'll disclose any difference between them.

**Target for a recommendation:** recall at turn 300 within 5 points of recall at turn 50, with no drop in task success. The check that could rule out any approach is its paired recall difference on the same sessions.

**My prior, for the data to confirm or refute:** if most misses are "present but ignored", retrieval (C) won't help, and the ledger (B) or a change in how context is injected is the bet. If most are "evicted", the ledger or a better summary is the cheap test to run first.

**What I'll build**
- A brief under `~/reviews/long-session-memory/` built with the brief kit:
  - a scoreboard table of all measured fields for every approach;
  - a chart of recall against turn number, one line per approach;
  - a stacked chart showing what share of misses fall into each cause;
  - a recommendation of keep, change, or run a further check.
- I'll score the brief with `brief-judge`, fix its weakest dimensions, and log the run with `skill-run-log`.
